Coordinate convention:
- All coordinates are normalized to [0,1] against the full image 1, never against a reference crop.
- The origin is the top-left image boundary; x increases rightward and y increases downward.
- bbox holds exactly two corners: top-left, then bottom-right, with x1 < x2 and y1 < y2.
- Return one tight box around the visible target. A partly visible target is acceptable when identifiable.
- Surface points lie inside this bbox.

Surface point selection:
- Select up to 5 points on clearly visible surfaces of the specified target; choose their number and positions yourself.
- Accuracy outranks spread and count: one accurate point beats five uncertain ones.
- Every point lies on an opaque solid part of the specified target in image 1.
- For open-frame, lattice or perforated structures, select visible structural members or solid components, never their openings or empty interiors.
- Prefer broad solid regions, thick members and solid joints, with visible material around each point.
- Avoid silhouettes, material boundaries, thin edges, holes, transparent glass, reflections and occluding objects.
- Any arrangement of the selected points is acceptable.
- Select the geometric center only when that exact pixel visibly belongs to a solid part of the target.
- Do not infer points on hidden surfaces.
- Check each rounded coordinate against image 1 before answering; keep only the points you can confidently attribute to the target.
